  "id": "Compound::DB01206 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Lomustine is implicated in the treatment of hematologic cancer through its inclusion in the Alkylating Activity category, which is linked to Mechlorethamine and Thiotepa, both of which are known to treat hematologic cancer. This suggests a shared mechanism of action involving alkylating agents, which are widely used in oncology for their ability to damage DNA and inhibit tumor growth. The presence of Lomustine in the same category as these agents underscores its potential role in similar therapeutic contexts. Additionally, the connection between Lomustine and Idarubicin, which causes gastrointestinal toxicity, highlights a broader biological relationship, though this path is less direct in its therapeutic effect on hematologic cancer. The integration of these pathways suggests that Lomustine may function through a common mechanism of action, possibly involving DNA modification, which is a key feature of alkylating agents. The evidence supports the hypothesis that Lomustine, as an alkylating agent, contributes to the treatment of hematologic cancer through its ability to induce DNA damage, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
